#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176394 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "00cf6180-070b-5ebf-af24-bc4c4b0bd94e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Complex Eigenvector of a Matrix Given a Complex Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80160f95-80be-522f-8844-1441952bbe1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2} + \\frac{3}{2}\\textrm{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174907} {:answer/id #uuid "873d4773-08da-5486-b7b5-aaa7f297134e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2} - \\frac{3}{2}\\textrm{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174908} {:answer/id #uuid "a1f6acfc-136c-54e9-8517-019e44660209" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}\\textrm{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174909} {:answer/id #uuid "cfc4711a-0d02-5df6-a284-7af1866acfba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174910} {:answer/id #uuid "37d1ccb4-aeb1-5143-b3eb-de273a1b06d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2} - \\frac{3}{2}\\textrm{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174911}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "37d1ccb4-aeb1-5143-b3eb-de273a1b06d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2} - \\frac{3}{2}\\textrm{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174911} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7577f76-cfcb-5bfd-9f25-ba7306152057" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174906}] :question/id #uuid "74f2c3c7-9e59-5362-99fc-fbdc0df5c116" :question/math-academy-id "q-38477" :question/problem "If $v = \\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of\n\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -2 \\\\ 9 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $λ = 3\\textrm{i}$,\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313278 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ad17d11-325f-5888-a5cd-61ee27f7a103" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Two Linearly Independent Complex Eigenvectors of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2587ce7f-9706-5e5d-a6c3-342c2829bca9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313280} {:answer/id #uuid "8d1af84f-e790-5068-9824-6806ba3c43b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313281} {:answer/id #uuid "303a7f35-fd09-52b5-966b-b4dc47f3c4fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313282} {:answer/id #uuid "ef08afdd-7f9a-5f5d-a465-b3dae78fb845" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313283} {:answer/id #uuid "7c9bd114-1ed4-5b29-b1c6-5bc9d139a300" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313284}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef08afdd-7f9a-5f5d-a465-b3dae78fb845" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313283} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8a33ffb-350c-5fb6-a6d3-ada3e904661d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313279}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2afbb4be-27ab-5a68-86cc-ed70968f1c05" :question/math-academy-id "q-38494" :question/problem "Let ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{1}\\end{aligned}]$ and ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ be linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ -5 & -1\\end{aligned}].$\nGiven that ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2+3\\text{i}$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ find the value of $\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,{\\lambda }_{1}=-2+3\\text{i}$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ -5 & -1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-2+3\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2+3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1-3\\text{i} & 2 \\\\ -5 & 1-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(-1-3\\text{i}){x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -5{x}_{1}+(1-3\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(-2+3\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{5}(1-3\\text{i}){x}_{2}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}(1-3\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=5,$ we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1-3\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, since ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2+3\\text{i}$ is a complex eigenvalue of $A,$ the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the  complex conjugate of ${\\lambda }_{1}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{2}=\\overset{―}{{\\lambda }_{1}}=-2-3\\text{i}.\n$$\n\nMoreover, the corresponding eigenvector to ${\\lambda }_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1+3\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}=\\frac{(1-3\\text{i})(1+3\\text{i})}{(5)(5)}=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343599 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "00cf6180-070b-5ebf-af24-bc4c4b0bd94e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Complex Eigenvector of a Matrix Given a Complex Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fec7827-bad7-547f-8389-4e4d0e10f480" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}-\\frac{3}{5}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343601} {:answer/id #uuid "07c591b3-2963-54f9-b371-6bcff25ea2d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{13}-\\frac{3}{13}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343602} {:answer/id #uuid "97bc9eb9-99fb-5f8a-bec6-4f6938aedf5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2-3\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343603} {:answer/id #uuid "2ce75948-bf18-5d34-9b4b-899ae78011ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+3\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343604} {:answer/id #uuid "29721852-3731-5992-bbbd-6aff9aebc555" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{13}+\\frac{3}{13}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343605}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "29721852-3731-5992-bbbd-6aff9aebc555" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{13}+\\frac{3}{13}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343605} :answer-field/id #uuid "a028c170-ff03-5d7a-a774-14680687814a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343600}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "44d22880-1719-5f05-b9a2-8b81c0330bc0" :question/math-academy-id "q-81030" :question/problem "If $v=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ 13 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1+3\\text{i},$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =-1+3\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ 13 & 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-1+3\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1+3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2-3\\text{i} & -1 \\\\ 13 & 2-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(-2-3\\text{i}){x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 13{x}_{1}+(2-3\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(-1+3\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=(-2-3\\text{i}){x}_{1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ -(2+3\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=-1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2+3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{-1}{2+3\\text{i}}=\\frac{-(2-3\\text{i})}{13}=-\\frac{2}{13}+\\frac{3}{13}\\text{i}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313313 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "00cf6180-070b-5ebf-af24-bc4c4b0bd94e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Complex Eigenvector of a Matrix Given a Complex Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "440d8e7a-64e3-530d-b388-b616283767e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313315} {:answer/id #uuid "06294dd6-235e-5814-83d7-7a32d002f6a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313316} {:answer/id #uuid "523df79a-9229-549e-b163-4ddb8de44152" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313317} {:answer/id #uuid "01e5e832-33e5-5be5-b594-58a317ab6fb4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313318} {:answer/id #uuid "ab46d392-035a-50a2-8df5-5137e4f2acb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313319}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "06294dd6-235e-5814-83d7-7a32d002f6a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313316} :answer-field/id #uuid "e28a7752-f6c4-5722-a84f-d4b0da47fca9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313314}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9a5ec6fe-789c-5b17-b058-b3b284c2cf3d" :question/math-academy-id "q-81031" :question/problem "If $v=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-\\sqrt{2}\\text{i},$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =-\\sqrt{2}\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{2}\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i} & 2 \\\\ -3 & -2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}){x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+(-2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(-\\sqrt{2}\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}(2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}){x}_{1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}(2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=-2,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{-2}{2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}}=\\frac{-2(2-\\sqrt{2}\\text{i})}{6}=-\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\text{i}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313320 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ad17d11-325f-5888-a5cd-61ee27f7a103" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Two Linearly Independent Complex Eigenvectors of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "004f98a5-338b-5285-a123-795d807ce7ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313322} {:answer/id #uuid "77b6e6f4-8011-5c4b-a32c-8e48b4f17eda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313323} {:answer/id #uuid "3e034af1-7a3e-56c2-8aca-cd2c4832bf9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313324} {:answer/id #uuid "4edab877-c126-5aba-a005-89744edada06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313325} {:answer/id #uuid "44e717ca-11c9-51f7-bfa7-273651a533d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313326}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e034af1-7a3e-56c2-8aca-cd2c4832bf9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313324} :answer-field/id #uuid "7df39464-1276-54bb-acf8-90f03b292028" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313321}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "60d3c079-e063-599e-bac2-8b40e845bc8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-81037" :question/problem "Given that ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{1}\\end{aligned}]$ and ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ are linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 5 \\\\ -5 & -3\\end{aligned}],$\nwhat is the value of $\\frac{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we must solve the characteristic equation $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}5-\\lambda  & 5 \\\\ -5 & -3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (5-\\lambda )(-3-\\lambda )-(-5)(5) & =0 \\\\ -15-5\\lambda +3\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+25 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +10 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a} \\\\  & =\\frac{-(-2)\\pm \\sqrt{(-2)^{2}-4(1)(10)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-(-2)\\pm \\sqrt{-36}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pm 6\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =1\\pm 3\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda =1\\pm 3\\text{i}.$\n\nNow, let's consider the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=1+3\\text{i}.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 5 \\\\ -5 & -3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1+3\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1+3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4-3\\text{i} & 5 \\\\ -5 & -4-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(4-3\\text{i}){x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -5{x}_{1}+(-4-3\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(1+3\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{5}(-4-3\\text{i}){x}_{2}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}(-4-3\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=-5,$ we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}4+3\\text{i} \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, to compute the second eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=1-3\\text{i}$, we simply compute the complex conjugate of ${\\overset{―}{v}}_{1},$ and get\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}4-3\\text{i} \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}} & =\\frac{(-5)(-5)}{(4+3\\text{i})(4-3\\text{i})}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
